" "q-97024/selection/d7ee2113c464f6f1e3d65efcd32292d1cb08110fdd6bc4aaaf109e697bca298c" "q-97024/selection/0eaef8be384a805b23cc958bff4810587bfbe85cb63b270150a6bb9948555fbc" "q-97024/selection/2cb31755e64e0b083b205e84b73b8023a1709f085ddfae755737e9e904bc0c9c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-97024" :question/id #uuid "c9ebaabd-16a1-529b-877c-4dfb3d8de6ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-97024" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/assets/ffbfa9e7aa3462d7f60e8d70a00e0a5c1f1044bd270911259a6a1f1ce6d5b30b.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the image of the triangle under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of the triangle:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the image's vertices are $(-1,1)$, $(0,0),$ $(-2,2).$ Notice that all three points lie on the same line.\n\nSo, the image of our triangle under the action of $T$ is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/assets/c26cdc9a92481b7e2a7f9e128656d42c093b5fbb8d2ca4d02dc3bb262ce64341.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-97024/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d1252e9-f2ac-5adf-9cf5-ef48e35fb747"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-97024"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-162200/selection/99d5aeb2f87bf6ef13f130429773b8288f79a42bed11813730ac0875b5e7a025" :answer/id #uuid "f10a5988-93ff-50e6-aac5-4bc31c71115c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "A triangle" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162200/selection/0f01227ab4132a1cf25abe47ace21e46fc0c901af076ff4c5dae675af9c522e9" :answer/id #uuid "b61b27bc-e86f-5025-90fe-510e1fecf08f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The origin $O$ of the coordinate system" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162200/selection/c2d1a5ac4ed1170ec4ba684e8dd01cdba007525d6370ddfd38066ec585122318" :answer/id #uuid "46dd404a-75be-562a-ac29-e454cdd77c3e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "A line segment" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162200/selection/f5b2bfafb2dc92d309ddb5e86bc50a427805dfd590fef68751ff0eb644197542" :answer/id #uuid "65aa1cb7-8757-560f-972d-1ce4bf7ca90b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "A point, different from the origin" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162200/selection/efff15d31c09e1f108daa7793309521088dfa694bb5914dcf15d35cd6340a394" :answer/id #uuid "53fb57c9-9633-5910-ac73-62b028f87b04" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "A quadrilateral" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162200/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "510e7c4c-1573-5703-b264-e1e135f71bf7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-162200/selection/0f01227ab4132a1cf25abe47ace21e46fc0c901af076ff4c5dae675af9c522e9" :answer-field/choices ["q-162200/selection/99d5aeb2f87bf6ef13f130429773b8288f79a42bed11813730ac0875b5e7a025" "q-162200/selection/0f01227ab4132a1cf25abe47ace21e46fc0c901af076ff4c5dae675af9c522e9" "q-162200/selection/c2d1a5ac4ed1170ec4ba684e8dd01cdba007525d6370ddfd38066ec585122318" "q-162200/selection/f5b2bfafb2dc92d309ddb5e86bc50a427805dfd590fef68751ff0eb644197542" "q-162200/selection/efff15d31c09e1f108daa7793309521088dfa694bb5914dcf15d35cd6340a394"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-162200" :question/id #uuid "b068ccdf-2483-5d2b-9c0a-f13dae9811af" :question/math-academy-id "q-162200" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/assets/da6c0fb32e62b6effe133c527fee528c47b6f1c8dc7dd1d77d7d04f42b4c3d2b.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the quadrilateral shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any linear transformation $T$ with associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., $det(T)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., $det(T)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nSince $T$ is the zero matrix, the image of our object under the action of $T$ is the origin." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-162200/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fea43b6-fd7b-5edc-b6b7-7f8b2cbab502"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-162200"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-162206"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/assets/e6d0ce147aec90c3840a893e36596fbadf981ff96559f65d055f4486406cca7f.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the quadrilateral shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any linear transformation $T$ with associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., $det(T)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., $det(T)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-1\\cdot 1-0\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a nonsingular matrix. Therefore, the image of our object under the action of  $T$ is a quadrilateral." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-162201/selection/0f01227ab4132a1cf25abe47ace21e46fc0c901af076ff4c5dae675af9c522e9" :answer/id #uuid "4437aa5a-a749-5c6d-8f3d-d31b01601abb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The origin $O$ of the coordinate system" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162201/selection/4b7649d656eb4e6f40e81460f6b84b104149aace596473d883fc9f11ea628ebc" :answer/id #uuid "f85eb3db-c70b-5e67-bd54-ecfc8961e9fc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "A rectangle" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162201/selection/c2d1a5ac4ed1170ec4ba684e8dd01cdba007525d6370ddfd38066ec585122318" :answer/id #uuid "8fd42b68-f6b9-5024-b293-d78a5df92981" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "A line segment" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162201/selection/f5b2bfafb2dc92d309ddb5e86bc50a427805dfd590fef68751ff0eb644197542" :answer/id #uuid "bcd14e6c-96a8-5e25-8afa-97f7ad9c26f8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "A point, different from the origin" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162201/selection/99d5aeb2f87bf6ef13f130429773b8288f79a42bed11813730ac0875b5e7a025" :answer/id #uuid "214d698e-c823-548f-ae58-51ab4fe4e6f5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "A triangle" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162201/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "beca3bfc-6192-5bd4-9d93-1a9d76036bc8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-162201/selection/0f01227ab4132a1cf25abe47ace21e46fc0c901af076ff4c5dae675af9c522e9" :answer-field/choices ["q-162201/selection/0f01227ab4132a1cf25abe47ace21e46fc0c901af076ff4c5dae675af9c522e9" "q-162201/selection/4b7649d656eb4e6f40e81460f6b84b104149aace596473d883fc9f11ea628ebc" "q-162201/selection/c2d1a5ac4ed1170ec4ba684e8dd01cdba007525d6370ddfd38066ec585122318" "q-162201/selection/f5b2bfafb2dc92d309ddb5e86bc50a427805dfd590fef68751ff0eb644197542" "q-162201/selection/99d5aeb2f87bf6ef13f130429773b8288f79a42bed11813730ac0875b5e7a025"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-162201" :question/id #uuid "2a34e82b-455b-568a-911e-4e41e8de38c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-162201" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/assets/cfcbd4b374456b4a5913289592d875a42caf81fd02984180e68e34c5b82ef905.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any linear transformation $T$ with associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., $det(T)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., $det(T)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nSince $T$ is the zero matrix, the image of our object under the action of $T$ is the origin." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-162201/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fea43b6-fd7b-5edc-b6b7-7f8b2cbab502"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-162201"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-162199/selection/0f01227ab4132a1cf25abe47ace21e46fc0c901af076ff4c5dae675af9c522e9" :answer/id #uuid "d221da97-895b-554e-a0d0-4530a8bc7dfb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The origin $O$ of the coordinate system" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162199/selection/f5b2bfafb2dc92d309ddb5e86bc50a427805dfd590fef68751ff0eb644197542" :answer/id #uuid "5da67961-9e35-5789-b3b9-477442337571" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "A point, different from the origin" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162199/selection/4b7649d656eb4e6f40e81460f6b84b104149aace596473d883fc9f11ea628ebc" :answer/id #uuid "1fe16775-3759-5a79-97ca-1f38d17cc42c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "A rectangle" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162199/selection/a46b9d9cb098f30c2bfb1712d5eac34a9a6a7683788497e7234aec27f048077a" :answer/id #uuid "d703169c-1e19-58cf-a9d1-b6cc5d645446" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "A pentagon" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162199/selection/c2d1a5ac4ed1170ec4ba684e8dd01cdba007525d6370ddfd38066ec585122318" :answer/id #uuid "b3ada3c1-d523-5656-bdd8-ea73b1f354ac" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "A line segment" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162199/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "7db89d2d-1270-5238-a5d8-8337010a987f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-162199/selection/0f01227ab4132a1cf25abe47ace21e46fc0c901af076ff4c5dae675af9c522e9" :answer-field/choices ["q-162199/selection/0f01227ab4132a1cf25abe47ace21e46fc0c901af076ff4c5dae675af9c522e9" "q-162199/selection/f5b2bfafb2dc92d309ddb5e86bc50a427805dfd590fef68751ff0eb644197542" "q-162199/selection/4b7649d656eb4e6f40e81460f6b84b104149aace596473d883fc9f11ea628ebc" "q-162199/selection/a46b9d9cb098f30c2bfb1712d5eac34a9a6a7683788497e7234aec27f048077a" "q-162199/selection/c2d1a5ac4ed1170ec4ba684e8dd01cdba007525d6370ddfd38066ec585122318"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-162199" :question/id #uuid "1f5f7e14-de4a-5b80-a0ab-301a0dcb6346" :question/math-academy-id "q-162199" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/assets/eb1247ddcaaf12a342aa036550ed4f9f7daaeb6037e7ebbab0de619bb6975dbe.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the pentagon shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any linear transformation $T$ with associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., $det(T)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., $det(T)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nSince $T$ is the zero matrix, the image of our object under the action of $T$ is the origin." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-162199/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fea43b6-fd7b-5edc-b6b7-7f8b2cbab502"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-162199"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-162145"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/assets/e2ab6906100a96a123b4b1520613f1e38fe2c7e77af390a8efe0596a2861727d.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any linear transformation $T$ with associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., $det(T)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., $det(T)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot (-3)-6\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a singular nonzero matrix. Therefore, the image of the triangle under the action of $T$ is a line segment." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-12423"] :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nA triangle $S$ has vertices at $(-1,2), (0,1)$ and $(1,1).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of $S',$ the image of $S$ under the action of $\\mathbf T?$\n\n- $(-2,0)$\n\n- $(0,0)$\n\n- $(2,0)$" :question/worked-solution "To find the image of $S$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under $\\mathbf T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of $S'$ are $(-2,0),$ $(0,0),$ and $(2,0).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I, II,  and III.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-12424"] :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -6 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n  Find the image of the triangle shown below under the action of $\\mathbf T.$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/assets/853fd7d5c09944a5bacd0c2b3453504f3fa1eea9d8d3b2c3bdd4f0f44889dc16.png)" :question/worked-solution "To find the image of the triangle under the action of $\\mathbf T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of the triangle:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -6 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -6 & -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the image's vertices are $(3,-6), (1,-2),$ and $(-2,4).$  Notice that all three points lie on the same line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/assets/3fd1073b2eaa5aa9c21e537fcc4dc6cf538ab2992c0732f64da50dbcb63d8fd4.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-12438"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/assets/89efcd0ecb51dac08a1102c5a347eea3459f7209de7cbac0a88b192259ca3b7e.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $\\mathbf{T}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ with associated $2 \\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $\\mathbf{T}$ is non-singular, i.e., $\\det(T) \\neq 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $\\mathbf{T}$ is singular, i.e., $\\det(T) = 0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nSince $T$ is the zero matrix, the image of our object under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$ is the origin." :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
